1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,190
Changing password files does not finish a database password change.

2
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:15,750
KeyTurn gives engineers a handover record: apps work, old access is refused, and queued jobs still finish.

3
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:21,910
Here is our real IBM Bob run, using KeyTurn version 0.1.

4
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:31,790
With KeyTurn’s custom modes and tools, Bob traced the credential copies, planned the change and ran the checks.

5
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:33,930
Every final check passed.

6
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:42,200
Latest check with KeyTurn 0.2.1 (read-only): Bob found the half-changed handover “not complete”. It changed nothing.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,130
Apps, connection pool and database must agree on the new credential.

8
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:55,050
This next demo uses version 0.2.1, operated by a script.

9
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:59,170
The files changed; the live password did not.

10
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,950
KeyTurn records the simulated completion claim as FAILED.

11
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:11,550
The gate refuses this app restart: the database would reject the new password.

12
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:18,610
We schedule a customer post, then start monitoring with a passing sample.

13
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:26,790
We change the database password, reload the pool and restart the apps.

14
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:34,580
Customers pass again, but three of ten sampled journeys failed during restart.

15
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,510
The record shows that interruption.

16
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:47,430
Next, KeyTurn recreates the apps, forces fresh connections, restarts the pool and runs the migration.

17
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:53,510
It also confirms that the post queued before the change was published.

18
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,130
The final checks pass: VERIFIED.

19
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:09,170
A later tracked-file change or detected service restart invalidates that result until checks run again.

20
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:19,050
The handover record shows the checks, refusals and measured customer impact.

21
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:26,050
Plain Bob left the live password unchanged in three short-request runs.

22
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:30,350
Bob with KeyTurn passed its one run.

23
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:35,680
Plain Bob also passed with an explicit outcome request.

24
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,410
Our value is reusable checks and a record.

25
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:48,470
Today: one deployment shape, covered commands and sampled customer checks.

26
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:51,230
No zero-downtime promise.

27
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:56,330
KeyTurn makes “done” a result you can check.

